1. Purpose
This document does two jobs. First, it states the requirements the Twilio public website is expected to satisfy, derived by inspecting the live site rather than from an internal specification. Second, it provides a test suite that a tester can execute end to end using nothing but a browser.
It is written so that each requirement has at least one test case, and each test case traces back to a requirement. Section 12 holds the traceability matrix.
2. Product Overview
Twilio sells communication and customer-engagement APIs. The public website is the top of the acquisition funnel: it explains the platform, routes visitors to the right product or solution page, and drives two conversion actions — starting a free trial and contacting sales.
The homepage itself is a single long-scroll marketing page with a persistent global header. Its content sections are, in order: a hero with dual calls to action, a platform narrative in four parts, a product grid of six cards, a multi-language code sample block, a customer results carousel, an analyst recognition band, a closing conversion band, and a large footer with a sources accordion.
2.1 Primary visitor types
Visitor	What they are trying to do	Success action
Developer evaluator	Confirm the API can do the job and find the documentation quickly	Reaches docs or copies a code sample
Technical buyer	Compare products, understand the platform, check pricing	Reaches a product or pricing page
Enterprise buyer	Assess credibility, security posture, and analyst standing	Reaches customer stories or contacts sales
Existing customer	Get to the console, the help centre, or the status page	Exits to the correct destination
Job seeker / press / investor	Reach company information	Reaches the relevant footer destination
3. Scope
3.1 In scope
- The English United States homepage and all global chrome rendered on it: utility bar, main navigation, mega menus, search entry point, and footer.
- All homepage content sections and their interactive behaviour.
- Link integrity for every outbound link reachable from the homepage, verified to the point of page load only.
- Locale switching between the six published languages.
- Responsive behaviour across desktop, tablet, and mobile breakpoints.
- Accessibility, performance, and SEO characteristics of the homepage.
- The unauthenticated state of any form reachable from the homepage — field presence, client-side validation, and error messaging — stopping short of submission.
3.2 Out of scope
The following are excluded because they require credentials, create an account, or generate commercial contact. No test case in section 9 touches them.
Excluded area	Reason
Twilio Console (twilio.com/login) and anything after it	Requires account credentials
SendGrid and Segment login destinations	Requires account credentials
Free trial account creation — completing the sign-up at /try-twilio	Creates a real account and requires phone verification
Contact sales form submission	Generates a real sales lead
Webinar and gated asset registration	Creates a real registration record
Newsletter or marketing consent submission	Creates a real subscription
Any authenticated dashboard, billing, or usage view	Requires account credentials
Backend API behaviour, message delivery, and telephony functionality	Not part of the website under test

Where a test case reaches a conversion form, the case ends at verifying the form renders and validates correctly. The submit action is never performed.


